=== (J) symmetry of C Gamma^(k) on the 128 of D8 ===
C gamma_a C^-1 = s gamma_a^T with s in {(1+0j)}
dim S+ = 128
  k=0: C Gamma^(k) restricted to S+ x S+ is {'sym'}
  k=2: C Gamma^(k) restricted to S+ x S+ is {'antisym'}
  k=4: C Gamma^(k) restricted to S+ x S+ is {'sym'}
  k=6: C Gamma^(k) restricted to S+ x S+ is {'antisym'}
  k=8: C Gamma^(k) restricted to S+ x S+ is {'sym'}
=> S^2(128) = wedge^0 + wedge^4 + wedge^8_+ = 1+1820+6435 = 8256 = 8256 ; wedge^2(128) = wedge^2 + wedge^6 = 120+8008 = 8128 = 8128
=> the adjoint 120 = wedge^2 occurs only in the ANTISYMMETRIC square: an so(16)- (hence so(12,4)-) equivariant symmetric map S^2(128) -> so(16) is zero.
